      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Authentication Methods\nHow does a service know who is\ncalling, and how do OAuth, Auth0,\nand the other methods differ?\nAuthentication means proving who\nyou are. A caller proves it with a\ncredential: a password, a key, or\na certificate. A standard such as\n[OAuth 2.0](https://www.rfc-editor\n.org/rfc/rfc6749.html) (Open\nAuthorization) defines the\nmessages that carry the proof. A\nproduct such as [Auth0](https://au\nth0.com/docs/get-started/auth0-ove\nrview) runs the standard for you.\nA program has no keyboard and no\nphone. It holds a secret or a\nprivate key, or its platform gives\nit an identity.\n\nThe map has four parts. Core Ideas\ndefines the words. Standards\ncompares the methods. Products\ncompares the services that run\nthem. Programs and Service\nAccounts shows how a program signs\nin, with Snowflake and Iceberg\nexamples.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-a32100b12583",
      "originalText": "Authentication Methods\nHow does a service know who is calling, and how do OAuth, Auth0, and the other methods differ? Authentication means proving who you are. A caller proves it with a credential: a password, a key, or a certificate. A standard such as [OAuth 2.0](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html) (Open Authorization) defines the messages that carry the proof. A product such as [Auth0](https://auth0.com/docs/get-started/auth0-overview) runs the standard for you. A program has no keyboard and no phone. It holds a secret or a private key, or its platform gives it an identity.\n\nThe map has four parts. Core Ideas defines the words. Standards compares the methods. Products compares the services that run them. Programs and Service Accounts shows how a program signs in, with Snowflake and Iceberg examples.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "authentication-methods"
      }
    },
