    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 7604544276,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-9e581436fa42",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1332.0,
      "y": 120.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 507.5,
      "seed": 361913,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Authentication and Authorization\nAuthentication checks who the caller is. [NIST\n](https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.ht\nml) defines it as checking the authenticators\nthat a subject uses to claim a digital\nidentity. Authorization decides what the\ncaller may do. In Snowflake, a sign-in method\nsuch as a key pair proves who the user is. The\nroles that the user holds carry the privileges\n([access control overview](https://docs.snowfl\nake.com/en/user-guide/security-access-control-\noverview)).\n\nHTTP shows the same split with two status\ncodes. A 401 means that the request lacks\nvalid authentication credentials. A 403 means\nthat the server understood the request and\nrefuses to fulfill it. Both codes come from\nRFC 9110, a standards document of the Internet\nEngineering Task Force (IETF) ([401](https://w\nww.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.\n5.2), [403](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc\n9110.html#section-15.5.4)). On 2026-11-15,\n`curl -i https://api.github.com/user` sent no\ncredential and got `HTTP/2 401` with the body\n`{\"message\": \"Requires authentication\",\n\"documentation_url\":\n\"https://docs.github.com/rest\", \"status\":\n\"401\"}`.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-9e581436fa42",
      "originalText": "Authentication and Authorization\nAuthentication checks who the caller is. [NIST](https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html) defines it as checking the authenticators that a subject uses to claim a digital identity. Authorization decides what the caller may do. In Snowflake, a sign-in method such as a key pair proves who the user is. The roles that the user holds carry the privileges ([access control overview](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/security-access-control-overview)).\n\nHTTP shows the same split with two status codes. A 401 means that the request lacks valid authentication credentials. A 403 means that the server understood the request and refuses to fulfill it. Both codes come from RFC 9110, a standards document of the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) ([401](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.5.2), [403](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.5.4)). On 2026-11-15, `curl -i https://api.github.com/user` sent no credential and got `HTTP/2 401` with the body `{\"message\": \"Requires authentication\", \"documentation_url\": \"https://docs.github.com/rest\", \"status\": \"401\"}`.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "authentication-and-authorization"
      }
    },
