    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 4606909437,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-35a34c2218ea",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1332.0,
      "y": 380.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 332.5,
      "seed": 445234,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Principal\nA principal is an identity that can prove\nitself to a service. It can stand for a person\nor for a program. The Kerberos specification\nuses the word for the users and servers in its\ndatabase ([RFC 4120](https://www.rfc-editor.or\ng/rfc/rfc4120.html#section-1.1)). Snowflake\ncalls a principal a user, and a Snowflake user\ncan stand for a person or for a service\n([access control overview](https://docs.snowfl\nake.com/en/user-guide/security-access-control-\noverview)). In AWS, a principal is an entity\nthat can perform actions on resources. It can\nbe a root user, an IAM (Identity and Access\nManagement) user, or a role ([IAM roles](https\n://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id\n_roles.html)). Service Account Names lists\nwhat each platform calls the identity of a\nprogram.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-35a34c2218ea",
      "originalText": "Principal\nA principal is an identity that can prove itself to a service. It can stand for a person or for a program. The Kerberos specification uses the word for the users and servers in its database ([RFC 4120](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4120.html#section-1.1)). Snowflake calls a principal a user, and a Snowflake user can stand for a person or for a service ([access control overview](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/security-access-control-overview)). In AWS, a principal is an entity that can perform actions on resources. It can be a root user, an IAM (Identity and Access Management) user, or a role ([IAM roles](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles.html)). Service Account Names lists what each platform calls the identity of a program.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "principal"
      }
    },
