    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 2783845321,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-ee977806d728",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1332.0,
      "y": 900.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 437.5,
      "seed": 221951,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Token\nA token is a short string that a caller shows\nin place of its credential. It replaces a user\nname and a password with one value that the\nAPI (application programming interface)\nunderstands ([RFC 6749](https://www.rfc-editor\n.org/rfc/rfc6749.html#section-1.4)). Three\nkinds appear in this map:\n\n- An access token lets the caller use an API.\n- An ID token says who signed in. OpenID\nConnect defines it.\n- A refresh token gets a new access token when\nthe old one expires ([RFC 6749](https://www.rf\nc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html#section-1.5)).\n\nA token that follows [RFC 6750](https://www.rf\nc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6750.html#section-1.2) is\na bearer token: any party that holds it can\nuse it. The holder sends it in the header\n`Authorization: Bearer <token>` ([RFC 6750](ht\ntps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6750.html#sect\nion-2.1)). A leaked bearer token therefore\ngives its finder the same access as the\ncaller, until the token expires.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-ee977806d728",
      "originalText": "Token\nA token is a short string that a caller shows in place of its credential. It replaces a user name and a password with one value that the API (application programming interface) understands ([RFC 6749](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html#section-1.4)). Three kinds appear in this map:\n\n- An access token lets the caller use an API.\n- An ID token says who signed in. OpenID Connect defines it.\n- A refresh token gets a new access token when the old one expires ([RFC 6749](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html#section-1.5)).\n\nA token that follows [RFC 6750](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6750.html#section-1.2) is a bearer token: any party that holds it can use it. The holder sends it in the header `Authorization: Bearer <token>` ([RFC 6750](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6750.html#section-2.1)). A leaked bearer token therefore gives its finder the same access as the caller, until the token expires.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "token"
      }
    },
