    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 6433571870,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-cde308f17d75",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1332.0,
      "y": 1160.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 542.5,
      "seed": 152562,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Caller, Issuer, Checker\nA method with a third party has three parties:\nthe caller, the issuer of the proof, and the\nchecker of the proof. Each standard has its\nown names for them:\n\n| Standard | Caller | Issuer | Checker | Proof\n|\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| OAuth 2.0 | client | authorization server |\nresource server | access token |\n| OpenID Connect | relying party (RP) | OpenID\nprovider (OP) | relying party | ID token |\n| SAML 2.0 | user, through a browser |\nidentity provider (IdP) | service provider\n(SP) | assertion |\n| Kerberos | client | key distribution center\n(KDC) | application server | ticket |\n\nThe names come from [RFC 6749](https://www.rfc\n-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html#section-1.1), the\n[OpenID Connect terms](https://openid.net/spec\ns/openid-connect-core-1_0.html#Terminology),\nthe [SAML technical overview](https://docs.oas\nis-open.org/security/saml/Post2.0/sstc-saml-te\nch-overview-2.0.html), and [RFC 4120](https://\nwww.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4120.html#section-1.\n7). With Basic authentication, no third party\ntakes part: the service checks the user ID and\npassword itself ([RFC 7617](https://www.rfc-ed\nitor.org/rfc/rfc7617.html#section-2)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-cde308f17d75",
      "originalText": "Caller, Issuer, Checker\nA method with a third party has three parties: the caller, the issuer of the proof, and the checker of the proof. Each standard has its own names for them:\n\n| Standard | Caller | Issuer | Checker | Proof |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| OAuth 2.0 | client | authorization server | resource server | access token |\n| OpenID Connect | relying party (RP) | OpenID provider (OP) | relying party | ID token |\n| SAML 2.0 | user, through a browser | identity provider (IdP) | service provider (SP) | assertion |\n| Kerberos | client | key distribution center (KDC) | application server | ticket |\n\nThe names come from [RFC 6749](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html#section-1.1), the [OpenID Connect terms](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-core-1_0.html#Terminology), the [SAML technical overview](https://docs.oasis-open.org/security/saml/Post2.0/sstc-saml-tech-overview-2.0.html), and [RFC 4120](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4120.html#section-1.7). With Basic authentication, no third party takes part: the service checks the user ID and password itself ([RFC 7617](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7617.html#section-2)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "caller-issuer-checker"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
