    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 7526492472,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-ee5bb296c654",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1332.0,
      "y": 1420.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 595.0,
      "seed": 613077,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Trust and Federation\nFederation means that a service trusts another\nsystem to sign users or programs in. The\nservice keeps the public keys of that system\nand checks every signature. Only that system\nhandles the password. For people, the result\nis single sign-on (SSO): one sign-in works for\nmany services ([SAML technical overview](https\n://docs.oasis-open.org/security/saml/Post2.0/s\nstc-saml-tech-overview-2.0.html)).\n\nAn OpenID Connect provider publishes its\nsettings at\n`<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration`\n([OIDC Discovery](https://openid.net/specs/ope\nnid-connect-discovery-1_0.html#ProviderConfig)\n). On 2026-11-15, Google's file held\n`\"issuer\": \"https://accounts.google.com\"`,\n`\"token_endpoint\":\n\"https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token\"`, and\n`\"jwks_uri\":\n\"https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/certs\"`.\nThe token endpoint is the address where a\nclient asks for a token. The `jwks_uri` leads\nto a JWKS (JSON Web Key Set), which is the\nlist of the issuer's public keys. On the same\nday, the JWKS of GitHub Actions at `https://to\nken.actions.githubusercontent.com/.well-known/\njwks` held 4 RSA keys. Each key has `\"alg\":\n\"RS256\"`, `\"use\": \"sig\"`, and its own `kid`. A\nJWT header can carry the `kid` of its signing\nkey, so the checker picks the right public key\n([RFC 7515](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc\n7515.html#section-4.1.4)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-ee5bb296c654",
      "originalText": "Trust and Federation\nFederation means that a service trusts another system to sign users or programs in. The service keeps the public keys of that system and checks every signature. Only that system handles the password. For people, the result is single sign-on (SSO): one sign-in works for many services ([SAML technical overview](https://docs.oasis-open.org/security/saml/Post2.0/sstc-saml-tech-overview-2.0.html)).\n\nAn OpenID Connect provider publishes its settings at `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration` ([OIDC Discovery](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-discovery-1_0.html#ProviderConfig)). On 2026-11-15, Google's file held `\"issuer\": \"https://accounts.google.com\"`, `\"token_endpoint\": \"https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token\"`, and `\"jwks_uri\": \"https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/certs\"`. The token endpoint is the address where a client asks for a token. The `jwks_uri` leads to a JWKS (JSON Web Key Set), which is the list of the issuer's public keys. On the same day, the JWKS of GitHub Actions at `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com/.well-known/jwks` held 4 RSA keys. Each key has `\"alg\": \"RS256\"`, `\"use\": \"sig\"`, and its own `kid`. A JWT header can carry the `kid` of its signing key, so the checker picks the right public key ([RFC 7515](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7515.html#section-4.1.4)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "trust-and-federation"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
