    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1833837293,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-ea45b1b2f7ee",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1332.0,
      "y": 1680.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 350.0,
      "seed": 957541,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Basic Authentication\nThe client joins the user ID, a colon, and the\npassword. It encodes the result in base64 and\nsends it with every request: `Authorization:\nBasic <encoded>` ([RFC 7617](https://www.rfc-e\nditor.org/rfc/rfc7617.html#section-2)). Base64\nis an encoding that anyone can reverse, so\neach request carries the password in readable\nform. The specification says that Basic should\nnot be used without enhancements such as HTTPS\n([RFC 7617](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc\n7617.html#section-4)).\n\nOAuth 2.0 reuses the format for client\ncredentials. The example in [RFC 6749](https:/\n/www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html#section-4\n.4.2) sends `Authorization: Basic\nczZCaGRSa3F0MzpnWDFmQmF0M2JW`. The value\ndecodes to `s6BhdRkqt3:gX1fBat3bV`: the client\nID, a colon, and the client secret.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-ea45b1b2f7ee",
      "originalText": "Basic Authentication\nThe client joins the user ID, a colon, and the password. It encodes the result in base64 and sends it with every request: `Authorization: Basic <encoded>` ([RFC 7617](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7617.html#section-2)). Base64 is an encoding that anyone can reverse, so each request carries the password in readable form. The specification says that Basic should not be used without enhancements such as HTTPS ([RFC 7617](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7617.html#section-4)).\n\nOAuth 2.0 reuses the format for client credentials. The example in [RFC 6749](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html#section-4.4.2) sends `Authorization: Basic czZCaGRSa3F0MzpnWDFmQmF0M2JW`. The value decodes to `s6BhdRkqt3:gX1fBat3bV`: the client ID, a colon, and the client secret.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "basic-authentication"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 4789845653,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-505032eaf8a3",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1320.0,
