    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 3408944472,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-ca255db41fa7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1940.8,
      "y": 2200.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 437.5,
      "seed": 234783,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Authorization Code Flow\nA person uses this flow to let an application\nact for them. First, the client sends the\nuser's browser to the authorization server,\nfor example `GET /authorize?response_type=code\n&client_id=s6BhdRkqt3&state=xyz&redirect_uri=h\nttps%3A%2F%2Fclient%2Eexample%2Ecom%2Fcb`\n([RFC 6749](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc\n6749.html#section-4.1.1)). Next, the user\nsigns in and approves. Then the server sends\nthe browser back to the client with a\nshort-lived code. Last, the client sends the\ncode to the token endpoint and receives an\naccess token.\n\nA client that cannot keep a secret, such as a\nmobile app, adds PKCE (Proof Key for Code\nExchange). It first sends a transformed form\nof a random secret, the `code_challenge`. It\nlater sends the secret itself, the\n`code_verifier`, with the code. The server\ntransforms the verifier, compares it with the\nchallenge, and denies access if they differ\n([RFC 7636](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc\n7636.html#section-1.1)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-ca255db41fa7",
      "originalText": "Authorization Code Flow\nA person uses this flow to let an application act for them. First, the client sends the user's browser to the authorization server, for example `GET /authorize?response_type=code&client_id=s6BhdRkqt3&state=xyz&redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fclient%2Eexample%2Ecom%2Fcb` ([RFC 6749](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html#section-4.1.1)). Next, the user signs in and approves. Then the server sends the browser back to the client with a short-lived code. Last, the client sends the code to the token endpoint and receives an access token.\n\nA client that cannot keep a secret, such as a mobile app, adds PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange). It first sends a transformed form of a random secret, the `code_challenge`. It later sends the secret itself, the `code_verifier`, with the code. The server transforms the verifier, compares it with the challenge, and denies access if they differ ([RFC 7636](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7636.html#section-1.1)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "authorization-code-flow"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
