      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Mutual TLS\nIn ordinary TLS, the server shows a\ncertificate. In mutual TLS (mTLS), the client\nshows one as well. A TLS 1.3 client sends a\nCertificate message only when the server asks\nfor client authentication ([RFC 8446](https://\nwww.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446.html#section-4.\n4.2)). The server then knows the caller by its\ncertificate. OAuth can use mTLS to\nauthenticate a client, and to bind an access\ntoken to the client's certificate ([RFC 8705](\nhttps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8705.html)).\nOn 2026-11-15, the discovery document of\nMicrosoft Entra ID listed\n`self_signed_tls_client_auth` among its token\nendpoint authentication methods.\n\nSnowflake lists five authentication methods\nfor applications: workload identity\nfederation, Snowflake OAuth, External OAuth,\nkey-pair authentication, and programmatic\naccess tokens ([authentication overview](https\n://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/security-a\nuthentication-overview)). Programs and Service\nAccounts describes them. In the [Kafka \u2192\nIceberg \u2192 Snowflake map](../integration/kafka-\niceberg-snowflake/kafka-iceberg-snowflake.md),\nproducers and connectors use client\ncertificates to reach Kafka.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-7b95ff49b1d8",
      "originalText": "Mutual TLS\nIn ordinary TLS, the server shows a certificate. In mutual TLS (mTLS), the client shows one as well. A TLS 1.3 client sends a Certificate message only when the server asks for client authentication ([RFC 8446](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446.html#section-4.4.2)). The server then knows the caller by its certificate. OAuth can use mTLS to authenticate a client, and to bind an access token to the client's certificate ([RFC 8705](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8705.html)). On 2026-11-15, the discovery document of Microsoft Entra ID listed `self_signed_tls_client_auth` among its token endpoint authentication methods.\n\nSnowflake lists five authentication methods for applications: workload identity federation, Snowflake OAuth, External OAuth, key-pair authentication, and programmatic access tokens ([authentication overview](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/security-authentication-overview)). Programs and Service Accounts describes them. In the [Kafka \u2192 Iceberg \u2192 Snowflake map](../integration/kafka-iceberg-snowflake/kafka-iceberg-snowflake.md), producers and connectors use client certificates to reach Kafka.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "mutual-tls"
      }
    },
    {
