    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 2210918062,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-b4ec5339db0c",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1332.0,
      "y": 3500.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 857.5,
      "seed": 867767,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Auth0\nAuth0 is a hosted service. Its documentation\ndescribes it as a drop-in solution that adds\nauthentication and authorization to your\napplications ([Auth0 overview](https://auth0.c\nom/docs/get-started/auth0-overview)). It\nsupports OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect, SAML, and\nother standards ([protocols](https://auth0.com\n/docs/authenticate/protocols)). An Auth0\ntenant is therefore an OAuth 2.0 authorization\nserver that someone else runs for you. In the\ntenant, you configure applications and store\nusers ([tenants](https://auth0.com/docs/get-st\narted/auth0-overview/create-tenants)). Okta\nannounced the purchase of Auth0 on 3 March\n2021 ([Form 8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/\nedgar/data/1660134/000119312521067561/d149091d\n8k.htm)) and completed it on 3 May 2021 ([Form\n8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1\n660134/000119312521146823/d133101d8k.htm)).\n\nThe table compares the standard with the\nproduct:\n\n| | OAuth 2.0 | Auth0 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What it is | A specification, RFC 6749,\npublished in October 2012 | A hosted service |\n| Who makes it | The IETF | Okta |\n| What you get | Rules for roles, requests,\nand responses | A tenant with users,\napplications, APIs, and a token endpoint |\n| How you use it | You write servers and\nclients that follow the rules | You create an\nAPI and an application in the console, then\ncall `POST https://{yourDomain}/oauth/token` |\n| What it leaves open | The token format and\nthe scope strings | Auth0 decides: `audience`\nnames the API, and a token for a custom API\nlasts [86400 seconds by default](https://auth0\n.com/docs/secure/tokens/access-tokens) |\n\nAuth0 has four application types: regular web,\nsingle-page, native, and machine-to-machine ([\napplications](https://auth0.com/docs/get-start\ned/applications)). A program uses the\nmachine-to-machine type. Client Credentials\nFlow shows Auth0 as the authorization server\nfor an Iceberg catalog.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-b4ec5339db0c",
      "originalText": "Auth0\nAuth0 is a hosted service. Its documentation describes it as a drop-in solution that adds authentication and authorization to your applications ([Auth0 overview](https://auth0.com/docs/get-started/auth0-overview)). It supports OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect, SAML, and other standards ([protocols](https://auth0.com/docs/authenticate/protocols)). An Auth0 tenant is therefore an OAuth 2.0 authorization server that someone else runs for you. In the tenant, you configure applications and store users ([tenants](https://auth0.com/docs/get-started/auth0-overview/create-tenants)). Okta announced the purchase of Auth0 on 3 March 2021 ([Form 8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1660134/000119312521067561/d149091d8k.htm)) and completed it on 3 May 2021 ([Form 8-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1660134/000119312521146823/d133101d8k.htm)).\n\nThe table compares the standard with the product:\n\n| | OAuth 2.0 | Auth0 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What it is | A specification, RFC 6749, published in October 2012 | A hosted service |\n| Who makes it | The IETF | Okta |\n| What you get | Rules for roles, requests, and responses | A tenant with users, applications, APIs, and a token endpoint |\n| How you use it | You write servers and clients that follow the rules | You create an API and an application in the console, then call `POST https://{yourDomain}/oauth/token` |\n| What it leaves open | The token format and the scope strings | Auth0 decides: `audience` names the API, and a token for a custom API lasts [86400 seconds by default](https://auth0.com/docs/secure/tokens/access-tokens) |\n\nAuth0 has four application types: regular web, single-page, native, and machine-to-machine ([applications](https://auth0.com/docs/get-started/applications)). A program uses the machine-to-machine type. Client Credentials Flow shows Auth0 as the authorization server for an Iceberg catalog.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "auth0"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
