    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 5299844034,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-0004ce55d3e7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1332.0,
      "y": 3760.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 437.5,
      "seed": 153699,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Okta\nOkta Workforce Identity is a platform that\nsecures the employees, contractors, and\npartners of an organization ([Okta](https://ww\nw.okta.com/products/workforce-identity/)).\nEvery Okta org, which is the Okta account of\none organization, has a built-in org\nauthorization server. It issues access tokens\nfor Okta's own resources. A custom\nauthorization server issues tokens for your\nown APIs, and you define its scopes, claims,\nand access policies. In production, the custom\nserver is an optional add-on ([authorization s\nervers](https://developer.okta.com/docs/concep\nts/auth-servers/)).\n\nA program uses an app integration with the\nsign-in method API Services ([client\ncredentials guide](https://developer.okta.com/\ndocs/guides/implement-grant-type/clientcreds/m\nain/)). To call Okta's own APIs, a service app\nmust sign in with a signed JWT. Okta names\nthis method `private_key_jwt` ([service app gu\nide](https://developer.okta.com/docs/guides/im\nplement-oauth-for-okta-serviceapp/main/)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-0004ce55d3e7",
      "originalText": "Okta\nOkta Workforce Identity is a platform that secures the employees, contractors, and partners of an organization ([Okta](https://www.okta.com/products/workforce-identity/)). Every Okta org, which is the Okta account of one organization, has a built-in org authorization server. It issues access tokens for Okta's own resources. A custom authorization server issues tokens for your own APIs, and you define its scopes, claims, and access policies. In production, the custom server is an optional add-on ([authorization servers](https://developer.okta.com/docs/concepts/auth-servers/)).\n\nA program uses an app integration with the sign-in method API Services ([client credentials guide](https://developer.okta.com/docs/guides/implement-grant-type/clientcreds/main/)). To call Okta's own APIs, a service app must sign in with a signed JWT. Okta names this method `private_key_jwt` ([service app guide](https://developer.okta.com/docs/guides/implement-oauth-for-okta-serviceapp/main/)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "okta"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1618867266,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-b07a1c521292",
