    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 5312870553,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-d48a5a521b98",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1332.0,
      "y": 4800.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 980.0,
      "seed": 211163,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Service Account Names\nThe identity of a program has a different name\non each platform:\n\n| Where | Name | What it is |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Snowflake | Service user | A user with `TYPE\n= SERVICE`: a service or application that\ninteracts with Snowflake without human\ninteraction. It cannot log in with a password\nor enroll in MFA ([user types](https://docs.sn\nowflake.com/en/user-guide/admin-user-managemen\nt#types-of-users)). |\n| Snowflake Open Catalog | Service principal |\nA user with `TYPE = service` ([External OAuth \nsetup](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guid\ne/opencatalog/external-oauth-configure)). |\n| Google Cloud | Service account | A special\nkind of account that an application or compute\nworkload uses, identified by its email address\n([service accounts](https://cloud.google.com/i\nam/docs/service-account-overview#what-are-serv\nice-accounts)). |\n| AWS | IAM role | An identity with specific\npermissions and no long-term credentials.\nAssuming it gives temporary credentials ([IAM \nroles](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/\nUserGuide/id_roles.html)). |\n| Microsoft Entra ID | Service principal,\nmanaged identity | The local representation of\nan app in a tenant, and an identity for an\nAzure resource ([apps and service principals](\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identi\nty-platform/app-objects-and-service-principals\n), [managed identities](https://learn.microsof\nt.com/en-us/entra/identity/managed-identities-\nazure-resources/overview)). |\n| Kubernetes | ServiceAccount | A non-human\naccount that provides a distinct identity in a\ncluster ([service accounts](https://kubernetes\n.io/docs/concepts/security/service-accounts/#w\nhat-are-service-accounts)). |\n| OAuth 2.0 | Confidential client | A client\nthat can keep its credentials secret ([RFC 674\n9](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html\n#section-2.1)). |\n| Auth0 | Machine-to-machine application | A\nnon-interactive application, such as a\ncommand-line tool, a daemon, or a backend\nservice ([applications](https://auth0.com/docs\n/get-started/applications)). |\n\nOkta, Keycloak, and Amazon Cognito use the\nsame idea. They call it an API Services app, a\nclient with service accounts, and a\nconfidential app client.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-d48a5a521b98",
      "originalText": "Service Account Names\nThe identity of a program has a different name on each platform:\n\n| Where | Name | What it is |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Snowflake | Service user | A user with `TYPE = SERVICE`: a service or application that interacts with Snowflake without human interaction. It cannot log in with a password or enroll in MFA ([user types](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/admin-user-management#types-of-users)). |\n| Snowflake Open Catalog | Service principal | A user with `TYPE = service` ([External OAuth setup](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/opencatalog/external-oauth-configure)). |\n| Google Cloud | Service account | A special kind of account that an application or compute workload uses, identified by its email address ([service accounts](https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/service-account-overview#what-are-service-accounts)). |\n| AWS | IAM role | An identity with specific permissions and no long-term credentials. Assuming it gives temporary credentials ([IAM roles](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles.html)). |\n| Microsoft Entra ID | Service principal, managed identity | The local representation of an app in a tenant, and an identity for an Azure resource ([apps and service principals](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/app-objects-and-service-principals), [managed identities](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/managed-identities-azure-resources/overview)). |\n| Kubernetes | ServiceAccount | A non-human account that provides a distinct identity in a cluster ([service accounts](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/service-accounts/#what-are-service-accounts)). |\n| OAuth 2.0 | Confidential client | A client that can keep its credentials secret ([RFC 6749](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html#section-2.1)). |\n| Auth0 | Machine-to-machine application | A non-interactive application, such as a command-line tool, a daemon, or a backend service ([applications](https://auth0.com/docs/get-started/applications)). |\n\nOkta, Keycloak, and Amazon Cognito use the same idea. They call it an API Services app, a client with service accounts, and a confidential app client.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "service-account-names"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
