      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Client Credentials Flow\nA program uses the client credentials grant to\nget an access token for itself ([RFC 6749](htt\nps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html#secti\non-4.4)). Only confidential clients may use\nit. The picture shows the five messages. The\nresponse holds no refresh token, so the\nprogram repeats message 1 when the token\nexpires ([RFC 6749](https://www.rfc-editor.org\n/rfc/rfc6749.html#section-4.4.3)). In message\n4, the API checks the signature, the issuer,\nthe audience, and the expiry ([RFC 9068](https\n://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9068.html#section\n-4)). It finds the public keys in the\n`jwks_uri` of the issuer's metadata.\n\n\n\nMessage 1 can prove the identity of the client\nin more than one way. On 2026-11-15, the\ndiscovery document of Microsoft Entra ID\nlisted `client_secret_post`,\n`private_key_jwt`, `client_secret_basic`, and\n`self_signed_tls_client_auth` as token\nendpoint authentication methods. A secret, a\nsigned JWT, and a certificate are three of\nthese ways. Stored Secret, Private Key, and\nMutual TLS describe them.\n\nSnowflake signs in to an Iceberg catalog with\nthis flow. The catalog integration holds\n`OAUTH_CLIENT_ID`, `OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET`, and\n`OAUTH_ALLOWED_SCOPES`. They become the fields\n`client_id`, `client_secret`, and `scope` of\nmessage 1. Snowflake's documentation shows a\ntest request. It sends\n`grant_type=client_credentials` and\n`scope=PRINCIPAL_ROLE:ALL` to `https://<accoun\nt>.snowflakecomputing.com/polaris/api/catalog/\nv1/oauth/tokens`. The response holds\n`\"token_type\": \"bearer\"` and `\"expires_in\":\n3600` ([check a configuration for OAuth](https\n://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/tables-ice\nberg-configure-catalog-integration-rest-check-\nconfig)). The [Iceberg in Snowflake map](../sn\nowflake/iceberg/snowflake-iceberg.md) explains\nthe principals and roles behind the client ID.\nThe Iceberg REST specification marks its own\ntoken endpoint as deprecated for removal. It\nencourages clients to set `oauth2-server-uri`\nto an OAuth server ([specification](https://gi\nthub.com/apache/iceberg/blob/apache-iceberg-1.\n12.0/open-api/rest-catalog-open-api.yaml#L190-\nL197)).\n\nThe token server can be another product. The\nsetting `OAUTH_TOKEN_URI` names the token\nserver of a third-party identity provider.\nWithout it, Snowflake uses the catalog as the\ntoken server ([CREATE CATALOG INTEGRATION](htt\nps://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/sql/c\nreate-catalog-integration-open-catalog)).\nSnowflake's guide for Open Catalog shows Auth0\nin that role. The steps for Okta and Microsoft\nEntra ID are similar ([External OAuth setup](h\nttps://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/openca\ntalog/external-oauth-configure)):\n\n- In Auth0, you define an API. Its identifier\nis the audience. You add permissions named\n`SESSION:ROLE:<role>`, and you create an\napplication. The client ID of that application\nis the identity of the program.\n- In Open Catalog, a security integration with\n`TYPE = EXTERNAL_OAUTH` names the issuer\n`https://<Auth0_domain>/`, the key URL `https:\n//<Auth0_domain>/.well-known/jwks.json`, and\nthe audience. The claim `sub` maps to the\nlogin name of a user.\n- The service principal is a user with\n`LOGIN_NAME='<client_id>@clients'` and\n`TYPE='service'`.\n- The program asks Auth0 for a token: `POST\nhttps://<Auth0_domain>/oauth/token` with\n`grant_type=client_credentials`, `client_id`,\n`client_secret`, `audience`, and\n`scope=SESSION:ROLE:<role>`.\n- A Spark client takes the same values as\nIceberg catalog properties:\n`rest.auth.type=oauth2`, `oauth2-server-uri`,\n`credential`, `scope`, and `audience`\n([connect with External OAuth](https://docs.sn\nowflake.com/en/user-guide/opencatalog/external\n-oauth-connect)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-5dd1a7e52330",
      "originalText": "Client Credentials Flow\nA program uses the client credentials grant to get an access token for itself ([RFC 6749](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html#section-4.4)). Only confidential clients may use it. The picture shows the five messages. The response holds no refresh token, so the program repeats message 1 when the token expires ([RFC 6749](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.html#section-4.4.3)). In message 4, the API checks the signature, the issuer, the audience, and the expiry ([RFC 9068](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9068.html#section-4)). It finds the public keys in the `jwks_uri` of the issuer's metadata.\n\n\n\nMessage 1 can prove the identity of the client in more than one way. On 2026-11-15, the discovery document of Microsoft Entra ID listed `client_secret_post`, `private_key_jwt`, `client_secret_basic`, and `self_signed_tls_client_auth` as token endpoint authentication methods. A secret, a signed JWT, and a certificate are three of these ways. Stored Secret, Private Key, and Mutual TLS describe them.\n\nSnowflake signs in to an Iceberg catalog with this flow. The catalog integration holds `OAUTH_CLIENT_ID`, `OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET`, and `OAUTH_ALLOWED_SCOPES`. They become the fields `client_id`, `client_secret`, and `scope` of message 1. Snowflake's documentation shows a test request. It sends `grant_type=client_credentials` and `scope=PRINCIPAL_ROLE:ALL` to `https://<account>.snowflakecomputing.com/polaris/api/catalog/v1/oauth/tokens`. The response holds `\"token_type\": \"bearer\"` and `\"expires_in\": 3600` ([check a configuration for OAuth](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/tables-iceberg-configure-catalog-integration-rest-check-config)). The [Iceberg in Snowflake map](../snowflake/iceberg/snowflake-iceberg.md) explains the principals and roles behind the client ID. The Iceberg REST specification marks its own token endpoint as deprecated for removal. It encourages clients to set `oauth2-server-uri` to an OAuth server ([specification](https://github.com/apache/iceberg/blob/apache-iceberg-1.12.0/open-api/rest-catalog-open-api.yaml#L190-L197)).\n\nThe token server can be another product. The setting `OAUTH_TOKEN_URI` names the token server of a third-party identity provider. Without it, Snowflake uses the catalog as the token server ([CREATE CATALOG INTEGRATION](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/sql/create-catalog-integration-open-catalog)). Snowflake's guide for Open Catalog shows Auth0 in that role. The steps for Okta and Microsoft Entra ID are similar ([External OAuth setup](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/opencatalog/external-oauth-configure)):\n\n- In Auth0, you define an API. Its identifier is the audience. You add permissions named `SESSION:ROLE:<role>`, and you create an application. The client ID of that application is the identity of the program.\n- In Open Catalog, a security integration with `TYPE = EXTERNAL_OAUTH` names the issuer `https://<Auth0_domain>/`, the key URL `https://<Auth0_domain>/.well-known/jwks.json`, and the audience. The claim `sub` maps to the login name of a user.\n- The service principal is a user with `LOGIN_NAME='<client_id>@clients'` and `TYPE='service'`.\n- The program asks Auth0 for a token: `POST https://<Auth0_domain>/oauth/token` with `grant_type=client_credentials`, `client_id`, `client_secret`, `audience`, and `scope=SESSION:ROLE:<role>`.\n- A Spark client takes the same values as Iceberg catalog properties: `rest.auth.type=oauth2`, `oauth2-server-uri`, `credential`, `scope`, and `audience` ([connect with External OAuth](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/opencatalog/external-oauth-connect)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "client-credentials-flow"
      }
    },
