      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Stored Secret\nA stored secret is a string that the program\nkeeps and sends. Examples are a password, an\nAPI key, a client secret, and a token. It is\nthe simplest credential. The Snowflake\nprogrammatic access token (PAT) is an example.\nIt is a time-limited credential that lets an\napplication authenticate without a password\n([authentication overview](https://docs.snowfl\nake.com/en/user-guide/security-authentication-\noverview)). This statement creates one for a\nservice user ([PAT guide](https://docs.snowfla\nke.com/en/user-guide/programmatic-access-token\ns)):\n\n`ALTER USER IF EXISTS example_service_user ADD\nPROGRAMMATIC ACCESS TOKEN\nexample_service_user_token ROLE_RESTRICTION =\n'example_service_user_role';`\n\nA PAT expires after 15 days by default and\nafter 365 days at most. A service user must\nname the role that the sessions of the token\nuse, and must be subject to a network policy.\nSnowflake shows the secret once, when it\ncreates the token. The program sends the\nsecret with each request: `Authorization:\nBearer <token_secret>`.\n\nSnowflake is phasing out passwords for service\nusers. A user with `TYPE = SERVICE` cannot log\nin with a password. The deprecated type\n`LEGACY_SERVICE` still allows one. The\nschedule gives August to October 2026 for\nstrong authentication for all users, and the\ndates vary by account ([timeline](https://docs\n.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/security-mfa-roll\nout)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-e12ea5603ad9",
      "originalText": "Stored Secret\nA stored secret is a string that the program keeps and sends. Examples are a password, an API key, a client secret, and a token. It is the simplest credential. The Snowflake programmatic access token (PAT) is an example. It is a time-limited credential that lets an application authenticate without a password ([authentication overview](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/security-authentication-overview)). This statement creates one for a service user ([PAT guide](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/programmatic-access-tokens)):\n\n`ALTER USER IF EXISTS example_service_user ADD PROGRAMMATIC ACCESS TOKEN example_service_user_token ROLE_RESTRICTION = 'example_service_user_role';`\n\nA PAT expires after 15 days by default and after 365 days at most. A service user must name the role that the sessions of the token use, and must be subject to a network policy. Snowflake shows the secret once, when it creates the token. The program sends the secret with each request: `Authorization: Bearer <token_secret>`.\n\nSnowflake is phasing out passwords for service users. A user with `TYPE = SERVICE` cannot log in with a password. The deprecated type `LEGACY_SERVICE` still allows one. The schedule gives August to October 2026 for strong authentication for all users, and the dates vary by account ([timeline](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/security-mfa-rollout)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "stored-secret"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
