      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Private Key\nWith a private key, a program proves who it is\nby signing a message. The service keeps only\nthe matching public key. The private key stays\nwith the program. Only signatures leave it.\nThe signed message is a JWT, and the service\nchecks its signature with the public key. [RFC\n7523](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7523.h\ntml#section-2.1) defines the use of a JWT as\nan OAuth 2.0 grant or as client\nauthentication.\n\nSnowflake calls this key-pair authentication.\nAn administrator assigns the public key to the\nuser ([key-pair authentication](https://docs.s\nnowflake.com/en/user-guide/key-pair-auth)):\n\n`ALTER USER example_user ADD KEY PAIR my_key\nPUBLIC_KEY = 'MIIBIjANBgkqh...';`\n\nThe client then builds a JWT ([SQL API authent\nication](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/develop\ner-guide/sql-api/authenticating)). The claim\n`iss` is\n`<account>.<user>.SHA256:<fingerprint>`, and\n`sub` is `<account>.<user>`. The claims `iat`\nand `exp` give the time of issue and the time\nof expiry. The token is valid for one hour at\nmost. The fingerprint is the SHA-256 hash of\nthe public key in DER (Distinguished Encoding\nRules) form, written in base64 with the prefix\n`SHA256:`. The client sends `Authorization:\nBearer <JWT>`, or sets\n`authenticator='SNOWFLAKE_JWT'` in the [Python\nconnector](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/devel\noper-guide/python-connector/python-connector-c\nonnect). About the Examples builds such a JWT\nwith a new 2048-bit key. The fingerprint has\n51 characters, and the RS256 signature has 256\nbytes, which is 342 characters in base64url.\n\nOther services use the same idea:\n\n- A Google service account signs a JWT with\n`aud` set to\n`https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token`. It\nsends the JWT with `grant_type=urn:ietf:params\n:oauth:grant-type:jwt-bearer`. The JWT lasts\none hour at most ([Google](https://developers.\ngoogle.com/identity/protocols/oauth2/service-a\nccount)).\n- Okta requires a signed JWT, which it names\n`private_key_jwt`, from a service app that\ngets tokens with Okta scopes ([service app gui\nde](https://developer.okta.com/docs/guides/imp\nlement-oauth-for-okta-serviceapp/main/)).\n- Snowflake Open Catalog accepts a key-pair\nJWT in the `client_secret` field of its token\nrequest ([key pair authentication](https://doc\ns.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/opencatalog/key-\npair-auth-configure)).\n\nA Snowflake user can hold up to two public\nkeys at once, `RSA_PUBLIC_KEY` and\n`RSA_PUBLIC_KEY_2`, for key rotation\n([key-pair rotation](https://docs.snowflake.co\nm/en/user-guide/key-pair-auth#configuring-key-\npair-rotation)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-2c35baf5aa80",
      "originalText": "Private Key\nWith a private key, a program proves who it is by signing a message. The service keeps only the matching public key. The private key stays with the program. Only signatures leave it. The signed message is a JWT, and the service checks its signature with the public key. [RFC 7523](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7523.html#section-2.1) defines the use of a JWT as an OAuth 2.0 grant or as client authentication.\n\nSnowflake calls this key-pair authentication. An administrator assigns the public key to the user ([key-pair authentication](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/key-pair-auth)):\n\n`ALTER USER example_user ADD KEY PAIR my_key PUBLIC_KEY = 'MIIBIjANBgkqh...';`\n\nThe client then builds a JWT ([SQL API authentication](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/sql-api/authenticating)). The claim `iss` is `<account>.<user>.SHA256:<fingerprint>`, and `sub` is `<account>.<user>`. The claims `iat` and `exp` give the time of issue and the time of expiry. The token is valid for one hour at most. The fingerprint is the SHA-256 hash of the public key in DER (Distinguished Encoding Rules) form, written in base64 with the prefix `SHA256:`. The client sends `Authorization: Bearer <JWT>`, or sets `authenticator='SNOWFLAKE_JWT'` in the [Python connector](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/python-connector/python-connector-connect). About the Examples builds such a JWT with a new 2048-bit key. The fingerprint has 51 characters, and the RS256 signature has 256 bytes, which is 342 characters in base64url.\n\nOther services use the same idea:\n\n- A Google service account signs a JWT with `aud` set to `https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token`. It sends the JWT with `grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:jwt-bearer`. The JWT lasts one hour at most ([Google](https://developers.google.com/identity/protocols/oauth2/service-account)).\n- Okta requires a signed JWT, which it names `private_key_jwt`, from a service app that gets tokens with Okta scopes ([service app guide](https://developer.okta.com/docs/guides/implement-oauth-for-okta-serviceapp/main/)).\n- Snowflake Open Catalog accepts a key-pair JWT in the `client_secret` field of its token request ([key pair authentication](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/opencatalog/key-pair-auth-configure)).\n\nA Snowflake user can hold up to two public keys at once, `RSA_PUBLIC_KEY` and `RSA_PUBLIC_KEY_2`, for key rotation ([key-pair rotation](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/key-pair-auth#configuring-key-pair-rotation)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "private-key"
      }
    },
    {
