    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 6162512669,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-57840e73529a",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1332.0,
      "y": 5990.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 875.0,
      "seed": 59087,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Platform Identity\nThe platform that runs a program can give it\nan identity. Then the program stores no\nsecret, and the platform issues short-lived\ncredentials on request:\n\n- AWS: an IAM role has no long-term\ncredentials. A session lasts 3600 seconds by\ndefault ([AssumeRole](https://docs.aws.amazon.\ncom/STS/latest/APIReference/API_AssumeRole.htm\nl)). Code on an Amazon EC2 (Elastic Compute\nCloud) instance reads the credentials of its\nrole from the instance metadata service\n([instance metadata](https://docs.aws.amazon.c\nom/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/instance-metadata-s\necurity-credentials.html)). Amazon ECS\n(Elastic Container Service) sets the variable\n`AWS_CONTAINER_CREDENTIALS_RELATIVE_URI` for\nthe same purpose ([container credentials](http\ns://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkref/latest/guide/fe\nature-container-credentials.html)). AWS Lambda\nassumes the execution role of a function when\nit runs ([execution role](https://docs.aws.ama\nzon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-intro-executio\nn-role.html)).\n- Google Cloud: you attach a service account\nto a resource. Code on the resource uses the\naccount as its identity ([attach service accou\nnts](https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/attach-\nservice-accounts)). Application Default\nCredentials checks three places in order: the\nvariable `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS`, a\nfile from `gcloud auth application-default\nlogin`, and the attached service account\n([search order](https://cloud.google.com/docs/\nauthentication/application-default-credentials\n#order)).\n- Microsoft Azure: a managed identity gives an\nAzure resource an Entra identity with no\ncredentials to manage. Microsoft Entra ID\ndescribes it.\n- Kubernetes: a pod, which is the unit in\nwhich Kubernetes runs containers, gets a\nsigned token for its ServiceAccount in a\nprojected volume. The token has an audience,\nwhich defaults to the API server. Its\n`expirationSeconds` defaults to 1 hour and\nmust be at least 10 minutes ([projected volume\ns](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage\n/projected-volumes/#serviceaccounttoken)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-57840e73529a",
      "originalText": "Platform Identity\nThe platform that runs a program can give it an identity. Then the program stores no secret, and the platform issues short-lived credentials on request:\n\n- AWS: an IAM role has no long-term credentials. A session lasts 3600 seconds by default ([AssumeRole](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/STS/latest/APIReference/API_AssumeRole.html)). Code on an Amazon EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud) instance reads the credentials of its role from the instance metadata service ([instance metadata](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/instance-metadata-security-credentials.html)). Amazon ECS (Elastic Container Service) sets the variable `AWS_CONTAINER_CREDENTIALS_RELATIVE_URI` for the same purpose ([container credentials](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkref/latest/guide/feature-container-credentials.html)). AWS Lambda assumes the execution role of a function when it runs ([execution role](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-intro-execution-role.html)).\n- Google Cloud: you attach a service account to a resource. Code on the resource uses the account as its identity ([attach service accounts](https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/attach-service-accounts)). Application Default Credentials checks three places in order: the variable `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS`, a file from `gcloud auth application-default login`, and the attached service account ([search order](https://cloud.google.com/docs/authentication/application-default-credentials#order)).\n- Microsoft Azure: a managed identity gives an Azure resource an Entra identity with no credentials to manage. Microsoft Entra ID describes it.\n- Kubernetes: a pod, which is the unit in which Kubernetes runs containers, gets a signed token for its ServiceAccount in a projected volume. The token has an audience, which defaults to the API server. Its `expirationSeconds` defaults to 1 hour and must be at least 10 minutes ([projected volumes](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/projected-volumes/#serviceaccounttoken)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "platform-identity"
      }
    },
    {
