      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Workload Identity Federation\nWorkload identity federation lets a service\noutside the platform trust the tokens of the\nplatform. The platform publishes its public\nkeys, and the outside service checks each\ntoken with them. Kubernetes publishes its\nconfiguration at\n`/.well-known/openid-configuration` and its\nkeys at `/openid/v1/jwks` ([issuer discovery](\nhttps://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod\n-container/configure-service-account/#service-\naccount-issuer-discovery)).\n\n\n\nSnowflake supports this as workload identity\nfederation (WIF), with AWS, Azure, Google\nCloud, and OIDC issuers as providers ([WIF gui\nde](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/w\norkload-identity-federation)). The feature\nbecame generally available on 14 August 2025\n([release note](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/\nrelease-notes/2025/other/2025-08-14-wif)). For\na pod on Amazon EKS (Elastic Kubernetes\nService), an administrator creates a service\nuser that names the issuer and the subject of\nthe token:\n\n`CREATE USER my_eks_service WORKLOAD_IDENTITY\n= (TYPE = OIDC ISSUER = 'https://oidc.eks.<reg\nion>.amazonaws.com/id/<issuer_id>' SUBJECT = '\nsystem:serviceaccount:<namespace>:<service_acc\nount>') TYPE = SERVICE;`\n\nThe Python driver reads the token from the\nprojected file: `snowflake.connector.connect(a\nccount='<snowflake_account>',\nauthenticator='WORKLOAD_IDENTITY',\nworkload_identity_provider='OIDC', token_file_\npath='/var/run/secrets/tokens/snowflake-token'\n)`. Snowflake gets the keys of an OIDC issuer\nfrom its discovery document and its\n`jwks_uri`, which must be publicly reachable\n([OIDC provider setup](https://docs.snowflake.\ncom/en/user-guide/workload-identity-federation\n#configure-your-oidc-provider)). The token\nmust carry the claim `iat`. Snowflake accepts\nthe signature algorithms RS256, RS384, RS512,\nES256, ES384, and ES512. It caches the keys of\nthe issuer for up to 60 minutes ([limitations]\n(https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/work\nload-identity-federation#limitations-and-consi\nderations)).\n\nA continuous integration (CI) job can do the\nsame. GitHub Actions gives a job a token when\nthe job has the permission `id-token: write`.\nThe job requests the token from the URL in\n`ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_URL`. The issuer is\n`https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com`,\nand the `sub` claim looks like\n`repo:octo-org/octo-repo:ref:refs/heads/main`\n([GitHub OIDC reference](https://docs.github.c\nom/en/actions/reference/security/oidc)). An\nAWS role trusts the token when its trust\npolicy tests the claims `aud` and `sub`. The\ncall `AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` then returns\ntemporary credentials, and it needs no AWS\ncredentials of its own ([AssumeRoleWithWebIden\ntity](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/STS/latest/A\nPIReference/API_AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity.html\n), [GitHub guide for AWS](https://docs.github.\ncom/en/actions/how-tos/secure-your-work/securi\nty-harden-deployments/oidc-in-aws)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-15efa1a61f5c",
      "originalText": "Workload Identity Federation\nWorkload identity federation lets a service outside the platform trust the tokens of the platform. The platform publishes its public keys, and the outside service checks each token with them. Kubernetes publishes its configuration at `/.well-known/openid-configuration` and its keys at `/openid/v1/jwks` ([issuer discovery](https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-service-account/#service-account-issuer-discovery)).\n\n\n\nSnowflake supports this as workload identity federation (WIF), with AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, and OIDC issuers as providers ([WIF guide](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/workload-identity-federation)). The feature became generally available on 14 August 2025 ([release note](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/release-notes/2025/other/2025-08-14-wif)). For a pod on Amazon EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service), an administrator creates a service user that names the issuer and the subject of the token:\n\n`CREATE USER my_eks_service WORKLOAD_IDENTITY = (TYPE = OIDC ISSUER = 'https://oidc.eks.<region>.amazonaws.com/id/<issuer_id>' SUBJECT = 'system:serviceaccount:<namespace>:<service_account>') TYPE = SERVICE;`\n\nThe Python driver reads the token from the projected file: `snowflake.connector.connect(account='<snowflake_account>', authenticator='WORKLOAD_IDENTITY', workload_identity_provider='OIDC', token_file_path='/var/run/secrets/tokens/snowflake-token')`. Snowflake gets the keys of an OIDC issuer from its discovery document and its `jwks_uri`, which must be publicly reachable ([OIDC provider setup](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/workload-identity-federation#configure-your-oidc-provider)). The token must carry the claim `iat`. Snowflake accepts the signature algorithms RS256, RS384, RS512, ES256, ES384, and ES512. It caches the keys of the issuer for up to 60 minutes ([limitations](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/workload-identity-federation#limitations-and-considerations)).\n\nA continuous integration (CI) job can do the same. GitHub Actions gives a job a token when the job has the permission `id-token: write`. The job requests the token from the URL in `ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_URL`. The issuer is `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com`, and the `sub` claim looks like `repo:octo-org/octo-repo:ref:refs/heads/main` ([GitHub OIDC reference](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/oidc)). An AWS role trusts the token when its trust policy tests the claims `aud` and `sub`. The call `AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` then returns temporary credentials, and it needs no AWS credentials of its own ([AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/STS/latest/APIReference/API_AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity.html), [GitHub guide for AWS](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/secure-your-work/security-harden-deployments/oidc-in-aws)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "workload-identity-federation"
      }
    },
