      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Choosing a Credential\nSnowflake compares its methods for\nservice-to-service use in a table. The table\ncalls workload identity federation the\npreferred option, because it needs no secret.\nIt calls External OAuth a strong option. For\nkey-pair authentication and programmatic\naccess tokens, it names the risks of\nlong-lived credentials ([choosing authenticati\non](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/s\necurity-authentication-overview#choosing-authe\nntication-for-service-to-service-applications)\n).\n\n| Method | What the program sends | How long\nit lasts | Snowflake's remark |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Platform identity | A token that the\nplatform issued | A Kubernetes token: 1 hour\nby default | Preferred option. Secretless. |\n| External OAuth | An access token, which the\nprogram gets with a secret or a key | An Auth0\ntoken: 86400 seconds by default. An Okta\nsample: 3600 seconds. | Strong option. |\n| Private key | A JWT that the program signs |\n1 hour at most | The credential is not exposed\nin a request. |\n| Programmatic access token | The secret\nitself | 15 days by default, 365 days at most\n| The secret travels in each request. |\n\nAWS and Google give the same advice for their\nplatforms. AWS recommends temporary\ncredentials from IAM roles in place of\nlong-term credentials such as access keys\n([best practices](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/\nIAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html#bp-wo\nrkloads-use-roles)). Google says that service\naccount keys are a security risk if you manage\nthem badly. It advises a more secure\nalternative whenever possible ([service accoun\nts](https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/service-\naccount-overview#what-are-service-accounts)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-49f976388572",
      "originalText": "Choosing a Credential\nSnowflake compares its methods for service-to-service use in a table. The table calls workload identity federation the preferred option, because it needs no secret. It calls External OAuth a strong option. For key-pair authentication and programmatic access tokens, it names the risks of long-lived credentials ([choosing authentication](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/security-authentication-overview#choosing-authentication-for-service-to-service-applications)).\n\n| Method | What the program sends | How long it lasts | Snowflake's remark |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Platform identity | A token that the platform issued | A Kubernetes token: 1 hour by default | Preferred option. Secretless. |\n| External OAuth | An access token, which the program gets with a secret or a key | An Auth0 token: 86400 seconds by default. An Okta sample: 3600 seconds. | Strong option. |\n| Private key | A JWT that the program signs | 1 hour at most | The credential is not exposed in a request. |\n| Programmatic access token | The secret itself | 15 days by default, 365 days at most | The secret travels in each request. |\n\nAWS and Google give the same advice for their platforms. AWS recommends temporary credentials from IAM roles in place of long-term credentials such as access keys ([best practices](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html#bp-workloads-use-roles)). Google says that service account keys are a security risk if you manage them badly. It advises a more secure alternative whenever possible ([service accounts](https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/service-account-overview#what-are-service-accounts)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "choosing-a-credential"
      }
    },
